-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THIS COLUMN IS FOR (plan 09 wave 4, stage 4B)
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- The console now mints a DIFFERENT `iss` per sign-in provider — the `issuer`
-- string of the `trusted_jwt_issuers` row that provider is bound to — because
-- `admin_identities`' uniqueness key is `(issuer, subject)` and a constant
-- issuer made two IdPs returning the same `sub` collapse onto one admin grant
-- (finding F24).
--
-- To mint the right `iss` the token minter has to know which provider
-- authenticated the CURRENT session. Better Auth does not record that:
-- `dist/oauth2/link-account.mjs::handleOAuthUserInfo` calls
-- `createSession(user.id)` with no provider argument, `account` is 1:N on
-- `userId`, and `user.email` is unique — so with two linked accounts nothing in
-- the shipped schema says which one just signed in. This column does.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- IT IS NULLABLE, AND EVERY NULL MUST REFUSE RATHER THAN DEFAULT
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- Every session live at the moment this migration runs has NULL here, and those
-- sessions reach the minter. `lib/auth.ts::sessionProviderId` THROWS for them
-- (`UnknownSessionProviderError`) and the surfaces translate that into
-- `console.error.session_provider_unknown` — "sign out and sign in again".
--
-- Defaulting instead would authorise a session against a provider that did not
-- authenticate it, and returning null rather than throwing would be worse still:
-- `dist/plugins/jwt/sign.mjs` spells the subject
-- `await getSubject(...) ?? ctx.context.session.user.id`, so a nullish return
-- silently falls back to the console's own user id and mints a token that
-- verifies and matches no grant.
--
-- A NOT NULL column with a backfill was considered and rejected: there is no
-- correct value to backfill WITH. The provider that authenticated a pre-upgrade
-- session is not recorded anywhere, so any backfill is a guess, and a guess here
-- is a guess about which admin grant a live cookie resolves to.

alter table "session" add column "providerId" text;
